# Configuration and security

Canonical: `docs/Personal_Rules.md` section 14.

- Settings live in ignored `.env`/`.env.local`; commit `.env.example` with placeholders only. Frontend reads `PORT` from `.env.local` before Next.js chooses its port. Backend and Electron read backend `.env` followed by `.env.local`; shell values take precedence. Do not ignore the local override file or overwrite it. Never commit secrets/connection strings or place secrets in `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`.
- Structured backend logs carry request/correlation ID. Log safe operations, status, duration, and error codes rather than full payloads.
- Never log secrets, tokens, personal/sensitive information, complete bodies, or upload contents. Redact sensitive fields.
- CORS uses an environment allowlist; no credentialed wildcard origin.
- Apply framework-supported security headers and explicit body limits. Added packages are pinned/documented.
- Validate upload type, extension, and size on the backend; generate filenames, prevent traversal, and keep private files outside the public web root.
- Protected operations enforce identity/permissions on the backend. Hidden routes and disabled controls are not authorization.
- Required audit history records actor, action, affected record, outcome, and Adelaide timestamp without secrets/unnecessary sensitive values.
- Use risk-based limits for authentication, public, and high-risk endpoints; do not break legitimate batches with an arbitrary blanket limit.
- Audit production dependencies before merge. Resolve high/critical findings or document mitigation and explicit owner acceptance.
- Follow `backend-reliability.mdc` for outbound timeouts, safe retries, health checks when deployed, and graceful shutdown.

## Standard variables

| Variable                                              | Use                                                                                      |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `DB_HOST` `DB_PORT` `DB_NAME` `DB_USER` `DB_PASSWORD` | SQL Server                                                                               |
| `PORT`                                                | Frontend listen port, loaded before Next.js startup                                      |
| `API_PORT`                                            | Backend listen port                                                                      |
| `NEXT_DIST_DIR`                                       | Optional project-specific Next.js cache/output directory; desktop dev uses .next-desktop |
| `API_PREFIX`                                          | Backend global prefix                                                                    |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_PREFIX`                              | Frontend prefix; initially api, matching backend                                         |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL`                            | Backend URL used by frontend                                                             |
| `CORS_ORIGINS`                                        | Accepted frontend origins                                                                |
| `MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES`                              | Non-file body limit                                                                      |
| `MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES`                                    | Upload limit when supported                                                              |
| `AUTH_LOCAL_TOKEN_SECRET`                             | Only for an agreed local-token design                                                    |
| `AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_USERNAME` `AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD`   | Optional one-time administrator bootstrap for an agreed local-login design               |

New variables go in matching `.env.example` and project documentation; add them to the guide if they become a shared standard. Provider-specific variables are documented only when the provider is confirmed.

## Authentication

- Owner specifies login, eligible users, roles, and permissions in `docs/ProjectInfo.md`.
- No login requirement means no auth system. Do not assume an identity provider.
- When required, agree on the approach and implement authentication during project development; do not defer it to a future integration.
- Modules use one shared implementation. Add only auth tables required by that design in schema `auth`.
- Pin/document added packages and provider variables. Test valid identity, unauthenticated access, permission checks, and denied operations.

## Local desktop configuration

- Honour configured `PORT`/`API_PORT`, for example 3005/4005. The fallback 3000/4000 applies only when unset. Validate range, distinct ports, and availability; do not kill/reuse unrelated servers or silently change the owner's ports.
- Reuse `frontend/src/config/startNext.mts`; programmatic env loading avoids unsupported Node env-file flags in Next dev-worker NODE_OPTIONS.
- Bind desktop-owned services to loopback and add the actual frontend origins to the child backend CORS allowlist while preserving existing allowed origins. Keep frontend backend URL/prefix aligned when adding API access.
- Backend credentials belong only in backend local settings and backend processes, never renderer/preload/IPC/NEXT_PUBLIC values. The Electron main process resolves configuration but never connects to SQL Server.
- Follow `desktop-electron.mdc` for renderer sandboxing, context isolation, permissions, navigation, optional native bridges, and owned-process shutdown. Local-only use does not waive these boundaries.

## Confirmed local login

- Username/password self-registration uses bcrypt 6.0.0, minimum eight characters, case-sensitive SQL uniqueness, and a server-assigned user role. Public registration cannot select admin.
- Backend checks protect management APIs; transactions preserve the last active admin. Updates carry rowVer; deletes are soft. Initial admin creation is an explicit local command, never an HTTP bootstrap endpoint.
- Session state is memory-only in the frontend and login is required after app close. Never store credentials/tokens in persistent browser storage or translation resources.
